      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i just had to do a rumenotomy on one of my heifers because of a blockage. can u give me a list of what i should be looking for in terms of recovery and signs of infection over the next week? also what should i feed her once she starts acting normal again?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A rumenotomy, a procedure to clear a blockage in the rumen, is a bit like a treasure hunt for the heifer's health. As you embark on this quest, keep an eye out for signs of recovery and infection, much like a vigilant guardian watching over a treasure chest. \n\nIn the first week, watch for a decrease in discomfort, a return to normal eating habits, and a gradual increase in activity. These are the golden coins of recovery. Keep an eye out for signs of infection, such as fever, swelling, or unusual discharge, which are the dark clouds that could signal trouble.\n\nOnce your heifer starts acting normal again, it's time to feed her a balanced diet, rich in nutrients, to help her regain her strength. Think of it as feeding her a hearty meal after a long journey. A good mix of hay, grains, and a vitamin supplement will help her recover and stay healthy. Remember, a well-fed heifer is a happy heifer, and a happy heifer is a productive heifer. So, treat her well, and she'll repay you with milk and loyalty.",
